1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,720
Whether you're having Chipotle or just mixing a salad from a grocery store kit, there's a good chance those ingredients come from Taylor Farms, one of the world's largest producers of fresh cut vegetables and salads.

2
00:00:11,720 --> 00:00:16,120
The company did around 7.3 billion dollars in sales last year.

3
00:00:16,120 --> 00:00:22,870
After years of consolidation, Taylor Farms now owns and operates nearly every point of the supply chain.

4
00:00:22,870 --> 00:00:27,940
The consolidation has camouflaged sources, but the benefit is good prices.

5
00:00:27,940 --> 00:00:36,115
It's all put in one big pot, and if that pot has now gone bad, that affects the entire downstream logistics food chain of all the restaurants.

6
00:00:36,115 --> 00:00:37,160
And it has.

7
00:00:37,160 --> 00:00:44,720
This summer Taylor Farms has been at the center of cyclosporiasis and salmonella outbreaks linked to its facilities in Mexico.

8
00:00:44,720 --> 00:00:50,430
While patient tracking data have linked the company to the outbreak, a direct link is yet to be established.

9
00:00:50,430 --> 00:00:58,355
The company has issued several multi-state recalls, and the FDA updated food safety guidelines for producers of fresh cut products.

10
00:00:58,355 --> 00:01:03,165
Is it just a coincidence that salmonella is happening at the same time as cyclospora?

11
00:01:03,165 --> 00:01:07,255
Americans should have full confidence in the food supply in this country.

12
00:01:07,255 --> 00:01:11,730
FDA investigates dozens of foodborne illness outbreaks each year.

13
00:01:11,730 --> 00:01:16,860
Taylor Farms is interesting because it continually denies that it had anything to do with this.

14
00:01:16,860 --> 00:01:23,340
Nobody's tested and found anything on our farm, but that happened because lettuce gets eaten and doesn't get saved.

15
00:01:23,340 --> 00:01:26,835
It's hard to pinpoint Taylor Farms' full market share.

16
00:01:26,835 --> 00:01:39,560
It operates in three different segments: selling salad kits in major grocery stores, servicing some of the biggest fast food chains like Taco Bell, and offering ready-to-eat products sold in delis across the country.

17
00:01:39,560 --> 00:01:43,290
Taylor Farms' competitive advantage is its infrastructure.

18
00:01:43,290 --> 00:01:48,360
It partners with hundreds of family farms for most of the produce; Taylor does the rest.

19
00:01:48,360 --> 00:01:55,440
That means everything from refrigerating, washing, chopping, packaging, labeling, shipping, and distributing.

20
00:01:55,440 --> 00:01:59,210
That's why the company has become so vital to its customers.

21
00:01:59,210 --> 00:02:03,445
40% of all salad kits at grocery stores are from Taylor.

22
00:02:03,445 --> 00:02:12,735
Taylor Farms was founded in 1995, and by 2010, even before its acquisition spree, it claimed to be the nation's largest salad producer.

23
00:02:12,735 --> 00:02:20,235
Over the course of the next 15 years, it either bought up or invested in at least a dozen companies domestically and internationally.

24
00:02:20,235 --> 00:02:24,870
Some of them were salad competitors like Earthbound Farm in 2019.

25
00:02:24,870 --> 00:02:33,170
Others were processing facilities like Club Chef, or tech assets like agricultural robotics company FarmWise in 2025.

26
00:02:33,170 --> 00:02:48,415
If they can promise institutions that have multiple locations all over the country that they can supply what they need on a regular basis, then for a restaurant chain, it's easier for them to deal with a major multinational company than it is to deal with local farmers.

